WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.170 --> 00:00:05.624
[MUSIC]

00:00:05.624 --> 00:00:10.150
Growing up, my dad gave my elder brother
and me any spare change he had, so

00:00:10.150 --> 00:00:14.531
we could buy the newest technology
of that time, a desktop computer for

00:00:14.531 --> 00:00:16.080
when we went to college.

00:00:17.630 --> 00:00:21.830
Every day, he would come out from work,
put the rupee coins in our hands, and

00:00:21.830 --> 00:00:24.668
we would run into our rooms and
put this money in our piggy banks.

00:00:26.590 --> 00:00:28.820
After a couple of years,
we had decent amount of money.

00:00:30.870 --> 00:00:36.290
It was August of 2002, I was 12 years old
then, sitting in my room and working on

00:00:36.290 --> 00:00:40.460
my homework, when my dad stormed into the
room, and he was upset like never before.

00:00:42.260 --> 00:00:46.040
He went straight to the piggy bank,
picked it up and smashed it to the ground.

00:00:48.600 --> 00:00:50.410
He then took the money and left the room.

00:00:53.000 --> 00:00:53.500
I was shocked.

00:00:55.150 --> 00:00:57.920
Unsure of what had just happened,
I went to the ground

00:00:57.920 --> 00:01:01.159
to pick up the broken pieces of piggy bank
and look if there was any money left.

00:01:02.530 --> 00:01:03.040
There was none.

00:01:05.320 --> 00:01:09.589
It was when I realized our family was
at the bottom of the economic pyramid.

00:01:13.012 --> 00:01:16.503
While we were there,
my dad had no stable job for

00:01:16.503 --> 00:01:19.570
years, he had no college degree either.

00:01:20.640 --> 00:01:23.370
So when this machine tools
workshop ran out of business,

00:01:23.370 --> 00:01:26.940
he went hustling to find a next job,
so he could run the family.

00:01:28.320 --> 00:01:33.145
With no history of borrowing, he was
labeled risky and costly, and was turned

00:01:33.145 --> 00:01:37.840
away by local banks, leaving him no
option but to take a desperate measure.

00:01:40.263 --> 00:01:41.637
It may sound cliche, but

00:01:41.637 --> 00:01:45.828
that broken piggy bank has become my
symbol of shattered dreams for my family

00:01:45.828 --> 00:01:50.360
and many families around the world trying
to break the vicious cycles of poverty.

00:01:53.030 --> 00:01:53.740
As a teenager,

00:01:53.740 --> 00:01:58.220
it was frustrating to see newspapers
claim the economy had grown many-fold.

00:01:58.220 --> 00:02:01.350
While we were at the very same
place despite years of hard work,

00:02:03.100 --> 00:02:05.760
I wondered if a family was
even part of the economy.

00:02:08.960 --> 00:02:12.240
Our financial struggles continued
until I graduated from college and

00:02:12.240 --> 00:02:14.100
found a job at a bank.

00:02:15.130 --> 00:02:16.430
Yes, a bank.

00:02:18.280 --> 00:02:21.590
The system that once rejected us
has now become a family's lifeline.

00:02:23.140 --> 00:02:23.870
Isn't that an irony?

00:02:25.420 --> 00:02:28.410
Or is it what banks and
businesses are supposed to do?

00:02:29.620 --> 00:02:31.620
Provide a lifeline to
the community around them.

00:02:34.060 --> 00:02:37.260
I worked at this bank and
provided a comfortable life to my family.

00:02:38.650 --> 00:02:41.800
But I couldn't erase the image of
broken piggy bank from my heart.

00:02:43.450 --> 00:02:48.342
I wondered how many families around this
world are taking desperate measures,

00:02:48.342 --> 00:02:50.762
only to see themselves stuck in a loop.

00:02:53.666 --> 00:02:58.235
So in 2016, I decided to tackle this
problem by creating a technology product

00:02:58.235 --> 00:03:01.490
that to provide micro credit
to unbank families in India.

00:03:03.240 --> 00:03:07.195
One of the first customers was Sunil,
wanted a loan for

00:03:07.195 --> 00:03:10.852
$500 to pay for his College fee and
continue with education.

00:03:12.500 --> 00:03:14.006
He had good grades from his high school.

00:03:14.006 --> 00:03:18.290
And earned a small income by working
part time at the food delivery company.

00:03:20.080 --> 00:03:23.440
He and his family lived in a shanty house
in the city outskirts of Bangalore.

00:03:24.640 --> 00:03:27.999
His dad drove a taxi and
earned $200 a month.

00:03:27.999 --> 00:03:32.602
When Sunil and his dad went to
the nearest bank to apply for a loan,

00:03:32.602 --> 00:03:34.120
they were rejected.

00:03:35.950 --> 00:03:39.720
The reason they were rejected was because
of the risky income profile of his family.

00:03:39.720 --> 00:03:42.610
And there was no profit to
be made of a small loan.

00:03:45.300 --> 00:03:49.870
Without technology, he could apply for a
loan from the comfort of his mobile phone

00:03:49.870 --> 00:03:52.920
by taking photographs of his documents and
submitting an online application.

00:03:54.050 --> 00:03:57.224
It was approved instantly, and
he could enroll in college the next day.

00:03:59.647 --> 00:04:01.210
Where the banks are unprofitable loan.

00:04:02.220 --> 00:04:03.730
Our technology could see an opportunity.

00:04:06.110 --> 00:04:08.960
Using mobile touch points we
reduce the cost structure and

00:04:08.960 --> 00:04:10.530
reinvented the business model of a loan.

00:04:12.030 --> 00:04:16.627
Our machine learning algorithms could
crunch through his bank accounts and

00:04:16.627 --> 00:04:20.377
mobile data and saw an opportunity and
provided a small loan.

00:04:20.377 --> 00:04:23.730
And the result He paid back
the loan in just nine months.

00:04:24.870 --> 00:04:27.780
We give thousands of such loans,
loans that help taxi drivers,

00:04:27.780 --> 00:04:30.700
small business merchants,
restaurant owners, pay for

00:04:30.700 --> 00:04:33.670
the kids school fee and
advance the family forward.

00:04:35.720 --> 00:04:37.870
Families that were traditionally
ignored by the banks,

00:04:37.870 --> 00:04:41.789
are now able to access loans,
dream big and progress in life.

00:04:43.970 --> 00:04:47.300
Today, Sunil is a proud first
generation graduate of his family.

00:04:48.770 --> 00:04:53.507
He has a job as a car sales
representative and earns $600 a month.

00:04:53.507 --> 00:04:56.285
He has access to formal banks and
health care and

00:04:56.285 --> 00:05:00.327
doesn't have to take desperate
measures to take care of his family.

00:05:04.206 --> 00:05:08.327
The impact I had on Sunil's life showed
me that technology can break traditional

00:05:08.327 --> 00:05:12.275
business models and benefit billions
of people at the bottom of the pyramid.

00:05:13.750 --> 00:05:14.780
There is hope.

00:05:14.780 --> 00:05:19.610
What was not possible many years ago when
my family suffered, is possible today.

00:05:19.610 --> 00:05:21.822
Thanks to mobile phones and technology.

00:05:25.728 --> 00:05:29.435
The upward mobility that Sunil saw is
a resounding example of how removing

00:05:29.435 --> 00:05:32.246
barriers to financial access
can turn things around for

00:05:32.246 --> 00:05:34.120
people at the bottom of the pyramid.

00:05:34.120 --> 00:05:37.610
And there are more than 4 billion people

00:05:37.610 --> 00:05:41.740
waiting at the bottom of the pyramid
making less than $1500 a year.

00:05:41.740 --> 00:05:44.110
And looking to overcome
barriers in finance,

00:05:44.110 --> 00:05:46.080
healthcare, education and employment.

00:05:48.140 --> 00:05:51.390
And it's not a problem specific to
India or other developing countries.

00:05:51.390 --> 00:05:55.380
Take United States for example,
over the last 30 years,

00:05:55.380 --> 00:06:01.380
the top 10% of the American Civil
wealth grew by 200% a staggering 200%.

00:06:01.380 --> 00:06:08.075
Whereas the bottom 50% remained at the
same place, what do we all do about this?

00:06:10.706 --> 00:06:14.600
From here on,
I can see the world going in two ways.

00:06:14.600 --> 00:06:20.149
One, companies and individuals don't
think about these 4 billion people, they

00:06:20.149 --> 00:06:25.078
think is the government's responsibility
to address this no, it does.

00:06:25.078 --> 00:06:27.400
Two, there's resonates with me.

00:06:27.400 --> 00:06:30.970
Well, as business leaders,
we embrace reality.

00:06:32.090 --> 00:06:34.770
We may not be able to get rid
of poverty completely, but

00:06:34.770 --> 00:06:38.990
we can do our best to remove
the systemic barriers in our society.

00:06:38.990 --> 00:06:41.250
And that is the path I choose
to go down in my career.

00:06:42.620 --> 00:06:45.980
If you're an entrepreneur,
I urge you to create technology solutions

00:06:45.980 --> 00:06:48.800
that would remove the economic
asymmetries our world is in.

00:06:49.980 --> 00:06:54.970
It is difficult, but not impossible to
create profitable businesses doing this.

00:06:56.210 --> 00:07:00.470
If you're an investor, I urge you to
bank bold ideas and commit to them

00:07:00.470 --> 00:07:04.230
over long periods of time and not just
look for short term opportunities.

00:07:05.280 --> 00:07:06.940
In addition to financial returns,

00:07:06.940 --> 00:07:11.690
it's time investors looked at economic
inclusion created by their investments.

00:07:14.220 --> 00:07:15.330
When I moved to the Bay Area,

00:07:15.330 --> 00:07:18.699
I was excited that I was stepping into
the technological future a perfect place.

00:07:19.800 --> 00:07:22.680
I saw self driving cars,
delivery robots and drones.

00:07:23.780 --> 00:07:29.430
But I also saw that while he was far
from reality, a far from perfect place.

00:07:31.100 --> 00:07:34.000
Last year, I took an Uber ride
from San Francisco Airport.

00:07:35.350 --> 00:07:37.840
I started a conversation
with the driver and

00:07:37.840 --> 00:07:40.406
realized he was a college
dropouts student.

00:07:42.899 --> 00:07:45.715
As we turn onto the palm drive,
he got excited and

00:07:45.715 --> 00:07:48.390
asked me what I was doing here.

00:07:48.390 --> 00:07:52.010
I told him I was a student,
his eyes lit up immediately and

00:07:52.010 --> 00:07:55.320
said he too wanted to come to Stanford.

00:07:55.320 --> 00:08:00.009
And here he is,
working two jobs a day to make ends meet.

00:08:02.750 --> 00:08:07.370
If I don't have conversations like those,
I fear I might live in a bubble and

00:08:07.370 --> 00:08:09.430
be blind to people living
from paycheck to paycheck.

00:08:10.760 --> 00:08:12.030
We all live in a bubble.

00:08:12.030 --> 00:08:15.061
We don't see janitors, drivers and

00:08:15.061 --> 00:08:21.139
the lives of 4 billion people breaking
the piggy banks every single day.

00:08:23.760 --> 00:08:26.671
Let's use technology that
can break barriers so

00:08:26.671 --> 00:08:29.519
people don't have to
break their piggy banks.

00:08:31.169 --> 00:08:32.018
Thank you.

00:08:32.018 --> 00:08:34.022
&gt;&gt; [APPLAUSE]

00:08:34.022 --> 00:08:38.779
[MUSIC]

